  "query_preview": "..An area of low pressure is centered across\nnortheastern Utah with light rain and snow showers along the\nterrain of central and northern Utah. Snow levels have been\nhovering around 9000 feet with accumulating snow on non-paved\nsurfaces observed at Wolf Creek Pass, although this snow has been\nmelting as the snow showers move out. Not expecting more than a\nquick dusting under heavier showers at elevation. A few lightning\nstrikes are possible this afternoon as conditions de-stabilize\nslightly, but most of this activity will remain in the form of\nbrief light showers across the terrain of central and northeastern\nUtah.\n\nThis low will move out of the area tonight with shortwave ridging\nmoving into the region tomorrow that will help temperatures rise\nback into the 60s across most valley locations and low 70s across\nlower Washington county. These temperatures will trend lower by\n5-8 degrees across northern Utah and SW Wyoming after the passage\nof a shallow cold front Monday evening. Guidance has trended down\non QPF totals as hi-res guidance comes into frame. Most of the\nhi-res guidance keeps all of the precipitation to our north, but\nthe NBM 50th percentile still has 0.1-0.2\" of QPF near the Idaho\nborder with lesser amounts as you head down the Wasatch before\ntapering off to nothing basically south of Utah County. A quick\ndusting to an inch of snow will be possible with the frontal\npassage as snow levels get down to 6000-7000 feet.\n\nWe will remain in a zonal flow with a broad trough across the PNW\nthat will keep conditions mostly dry with near normal\ntemperatures. Being on the periphery of this trough will keep the\ndoor open for some grazing shortwaves. Guidance is starting to\npick up on one of these Tuesday night that could bring another\nbrief grazing of valley rain and mountain snow across northern\nUtah/SW Wyoming, but this will be brief with limited precipitation\ntotals likely. The NBM 90th percentile gets as high as 0.5\" with\nthis round of precipitation, but the 50th keeps totals <0.25\".\n\nA more substantial storm starts to emerge towards the end of the\nweek and into the weekend as the aforementioned broad trough\nstarts to shift further south and begins to pull in deep moisture\nfrom the southern jet stream. The timing of this will be key to\nwhen and where the heaviest precipitation sets up. Guidance seems\nto have slowed down today with a bulk of the activity holding off\nuntil the ladder half of the weekend as opposed to the first half\nthat was being advertised the past couple of days. 50% of the\nensemble members keeps this trough across the west for several\ndays. This will increase our risk of flash flooding, particularly\nis the trough and heaviest moisture tap merge across the Great\nBasin.",
